# Frequency-Domain Analysis of Doppler Twinkling Signals

## Overview

This project investigates the frequency-domain characteristics of color Doppler twinkling in polymethyl methacrylate (PMMA) markers. Twinkling PMMA signals were compared with non-twinkling metal signals in the beamformed RF and IQ domains.

## Motivation

Color Doppler twinkling can improve ultrasound visualization of highly reflective targets, including biopsy markers. However, the signal characteristics responsible for the artifact remain incompletely understood.

This project examines the signal before formation of the final Doppler image. The goal is to identify spectral and temporal features that distinguish twinkling from a stable reflector.

## Methods

- Verasonics V1 ultrasound system
- PMMA twinkling marker and non-twinkling metal target
- Plane-wave Doppler transmission
- 4 MHz Doppler transmit frequency
- 2.5 kHz pulse repetition frequency
- Delay-and-sum beamforming using MUST
- IQ demodulation
- Wall filtering
- RF and IQ frequency-spectrum analysis
- Pulse-wise and frame-wise variability analysis

## Signal-Processing Framework

![Signal-processing framework](Picture7.svg)

Raw channel RF data were processed through beamforming, IQ demodulation, wall filtering, and Doppler estimation. RF and IQ signals were evaluated at different stages to determine how twinkling-related characteristics developed through the processing chain.

## Temporal Signal Analysis

![Beamformed RF signal analysis](Picture8.svg)

The PMMA marker showed greater temporal signal variation than metal. Metal maintained a relatively stable response across repeated measurements, whereas PMMA showed larger changes in RF and IQ magnitude.

## RF Frequency-Domain Analysis

![RF frequency-spectrum analysis](Picture9.svg)

The beamformed RF spectrum showed clear differences between PMMA and metal. After wall filtering, metal maintained a relatively smooth and stable spectral response. PMMA retained greater residual energy and showed broader and more irregular spectral content.

Normalization reduced differences caused by overall signal magnitude and allowed comparison of the spectral distributions.

## IQ Frequency-Domain Analysis

![IQ frequency-spectrum analysis](Picture4.svg)

IQ demodulation shifted the RF spectral content toward baseband. The difference between PMMA and metal remained visible after demodulation. PMMA showed greater spectral variation, whereas metal maintained a more stable spectral response.

## Key Findings

- PMMA showed greater temporal variability than non-twinkling metal.
- Wall filtering enhanced differences associated with the twinkling response.
- PMMA showed broader and more irregular RF spectral content.
- Spectral differences remained after normalization.
- IQ analysis preserved the spectral differences observed in the RF domain.
- Pulse-to-pulse variability was substantially greater for twinkling PMMA than for metal.
- Temporal and spectral features provided complementary information about the twinkling response.

## Significance

The results show that Doppler twinkling has measurable temporal and spectral characteristics before formation of the final color Doppler image.

Frequency-domain analysis provides a way to characterize these differences directly from RF and IQ signals. These signal-level features may help improve quantitative twinkling detection and guide the development of ultrasound-visible markers.

